1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,190
In a nineteen forty-one beauty salon,

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:04,930
a row of women sit
under metal helmets,

3
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:07,810
reading while hot
air dries their hair.

4
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:09,950
It looks like a modern routine,

5
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:13,030
but the first hair dryer
was not a helmet at all.

6
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:16,450
It was a bonnet connected
by a tube to a gas stove.

7
00:00:16,989 --> 00:00:20,689
Between that stove and the dryer
in a modern bathroom lie more

8
00:00:20,729 --> 00:00:22,479
than a hundred years of tinkering,

9
00:00:22,669 --> 00:00:26,599
some dangerous mistakes,
and one very noisy salon.

10
00:00:27,438 --> 00:00:28,998
This is the story of the hair dryer,

11
00:00:29,038 --> 00:00:31,788
and how it moved from
the salon to the home.

12
00:00:38,333 --> 00:00:40,823
Long before anyone dried
hair with a machine,

13
00:00:41,153 --> 00:00:42,523
people shaped it with heat.

14
00:00:43,033 --> 00:00:46,613
The ancient Egyptians are said
to have worn elaborate wigs and

15
00:00:46,653 --> 00:00:49,683
used a bronze tool that
could be warmed to curl hair.

16
00:00:50,153 --> 00:00:53,493
The pictures here come from a
film of about nineteen fifty that

17
00:00:53,533 --> 00:00:56,283
staged hairstyles through
the ages with actors,

18
00:00:56,483 --> 00:00:58,943
so they are stand-ins, not records.

19
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:02,880
The Romans are thought to have used
a form of curling tongs called

20
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:04,010
a calamistrum.

21
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:06,790
Examples have been found at Pompeii.

22
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:09,340
Heat was the secret to
making hair hold a curl,

23
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:10,750
and it still is.

24
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:14,500
But heat took skill, a fire,
and a great deal of patience,

25
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:17,210
from the hairdresser
and from the customer.

26
00:01:17,678 --> 00:01:21,198
By the sixteen hundreds,
hairdressers used pipe-clay curlers

27
00:01:21,238 --> 00:01:23,448
that were heated in
an oven before use.

28
00:01:23,938 --> 00:01:26,838
Another method wrapped hair around
wooden sticks that had been

29
00:01:26,878 --> 00:01:28,668
boiled or warmed first.

30
00:01:29,118 --> 00:01:32,498
Each section had to be curled
and then left to dry before the

31
00:01:32,538 --> 00:01:33,728
next one could begin.

32
00:01:34,265 --> 00:01:36,245
Rollers were often left in overnight,

33
00:01:36,285 --> 00:01:39,035
so the curls could
set as the hair dried.

34
00:01:39,405 --> 00:01:41,795
Drying was simply
something that took hours.

35
00:01:42,396 --> 00:01:44,306
Then, in eighteen seventy-two,

36
00:01:44,536 --> 00:01:48,286
a French hairdresser named Marcel
Grateau caused a sensation.

37
00:01:48,676 --> 00:01:51,636
He used heated tongs to
press soft waves into hair,

38
00:01:51,676 --> 00:01:53,286
instead of tight curls.

39
00:01:53,716 --> 00:01:55,946
It became known as the Marcel wave.

40
00:01:56,396 --> 00:01:58,176
Accounts differ on the exact year,

41
00:01:58,216 --> 00:02:00,666
and some say eighteen seventy-five.

42
00:02:01,483 --> 00:02:04,563
The tongs were heated over a gas
burner or placed in the fire,

43
00:02:04,603 --> 00:02:06,273
and that was risky.

44
00:02:06,763 --> 00:02:09,893
Tongs that overheated could
singe the customer's head.

45
00:02:10,418 --> 00:02:12,868
Grateau later moved
to the United States,

46
00:02:13,138 --> 00:02:16,598
changed his name, and took out
a patent for a curling iron in

47
00:02:16,638 --> 00:02:17,608
nineteen oh five.

48
00:02:18,218 --> 00:02:21,528
In nineteen eighteen, he
patented an electric version.

49
00:02:22,163 --> 00:02:23,213
In nineteen fourteen,

50
00:02:23,623 --> 00:02:27,143
a patent for a heated comb for
drying hair was granted to Emilia

51
00:02:27,183 --> 00:02:29,193
Baum in the United States.

52
00:02:29,503 --> 00:02:31,293
It could dry a little at a time,

53
00:02:31,483 --> 00:02:33,433
but nothing could dry a whole head.

54
00:02:33,845 --> 00:02:35,875
So by the end of the
nineteenth century,

55
00:02:36,145 --> 00:02:38,145
hairdressers had a
fashionable heat tool,

56
00:02:38,185 --> 00:02:41,215
and a growing number of
customers with wet hair.

57
00:02:41,565 --> 00:02:44,515
What they did not have was
any way to dry it faster.

58
00:02:45,045 --> 00:02:48,775
Sources describe an odd workaround
around the turn of the century:

59
00:02:49,145 --> 00:02:51,975
people used vacuum cleaners
to dry their hair.

60
00:02:52,105 --> 00:02:54,435
We could not confirm
how common that was.

61
00:02:55,047 --> 00:02:57,187
That was the problem
waiting to be solved,

62
00:02:57,227 --> 00:03:00,507
and one French hairdresser,
with a gas stove in his shop,

63
00:03:00,547 --> 00:03:02,077
was about to try.

64
00:03:08,733 --> 00:03:12,163
For most of history, wet hair
simply dried in the air.

65
00:03:12,513 --> 00:03:15,013
That began to change in
the late eighteen hundreds,

66
00:03:15,053 --> 00:03:15,863
in France.

67
00:03:16,502 --> 00:03:17,672
In eighteen eighty-eight,

68
00:03:18,082 --> 00:03:21,862
a French hairdresser named
Alexandre Godefroy proposed covering

69
00:03:21,902 --> 00:03:26,162
a damp head with a bonnet linked
by a tube to the hot air outlet

70
00:03:26,202 --> 00:03:27,352
of a gas stove.

71
00:03:27,862 --> 00:03:30,452
Some sources give the
year as eighteen ninety.

72
00:03:31,149 --> 00:03:34,539
It was a large, seated machine
made for his own salon.

73
00:03:34,849 --> 00:03:38,579
It was not portable, and the
customer had to sit under it.

74
00:03:38,789 --> 00:03:42,279
His idea set off a number of
variations around the world.

75
00:03:42,658 --> 00:03:46,618
In nineteen eleven, an Armenian
American inventor named Gabriel

76
00:03:46,658 --> 00:03:50,438
Kazanjian became the first person
to patent a hair dryer in the

77
00:03:50,478 --> 00:03:51,548
United States.

78
00:03:51,738 --> 00:03:56,348
We found no film of Godefroy's
bonnet or of Kazanjian's dryer.

79
00:03:56,738 --> 00:03:59,468
The pictures in this
chapter are stand-ins:

80
00:03:59,778 --> 00:04:02,078
salon scenes filmed in
nineteen forty-one,

81
00:04:02,118 --> 00:04:04,908
when the technology
was already far along.

82
00:04:05,328 --> 00:04:08,428
For a while, the hair dryer was
something you went to a salon

83
00:04:08,468 --> 00:04:09,318
to use.

84
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:19,560
Around nineteen twenty, hair
dryers began to go on sale in a

85
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:20,790
handheld form.

86
00:04:21,280 --> 00:04:24,340
Sources credit the National
Stamping and Electric Works,

87
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:28,580
under the White Cross brand, and
later the Racine Universal Motor

88
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:31,110
Company and the
Hamilton Beach company,

89
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:34,350
for making the motor and the
fan small enough to hold.

90
00:04:34,809 --> 00:04:37,319
These dryers were not
easy to live with.

91
00:04:37,429 --> 00:04:39,049
They often weighed about two pounds,

92
00:04:39,089 --> 00:04:41,699
and they ran at around
a hundred watts,

93
00:04:42,009 --> 00:04:43,999
so hair took a long time to dry.

94
00:04:44,389 --> 00:04:47,359
A modern dryer can use up
to two thousand watts.

95
00:04:47,918 --> 00:04:49,548
They could also be dangerous.

96
00:04:50,038 --> 00:04:53,798
One source describes hundreds of
electrocution accidents involving

97
00:04:53,838 --> 00:04:56,668
dryers in the middle of
the twentieth century.

98
00:04:56,998 --> 00:04:58,708
We could not confirm the number.

99
00:04:59,248 --> 00:05:01,238
Even so, the idea spread.

100
00:05:01,808 --> 00:05:03,978
Salons kept their helmet dryers,

101
00:05:04,068 --> 00:05:06,998
set in a row so that customers
could read magazines,

102
00:05:07,348 --> 00:05:10,238
or shout to each other over
the sound of the fans.

103
00:05:10,408 --> 00:05:13,188
We have no film of the
early handheld dryers,

104
00:05:13,228 --> 00:05:16,408
so the pictures in this chapter
come from a nineteen fifty-nine

105
00:05:16,448 --> 00:05:18,958
film about barbers and beauticians.

106
00:05:32,633 --> 00:05:36,383
By the nineteen forties, a trip to
the beauty salon meant rollers,

107
00:05:36,733 --> 00:05:39,203
a row of metal hoods and a long wait.

108
00:05:39,813 --> 00:05:43,283
Hot air blew from small openings
around the inside of the hood,

109
00:05:43,393 --> 00:05:44,963
so the hair dried evenly.

110
00:05:45,467 --> 00:05:49,177
The dryer also changed how
often people washed their hair.

111
00:05:49,607 --> 00:05:53,137
One writer notes that it sparked
a new focus on hair hygiene,

112
00:05:53,267 --> 00:05:55,457
with people washing more regularly,

113
00:05:55,607 --> 00:05:57,797
which was good news
for shampoo makers.

114
00:05:58,547 --> 00:06:02,317
In nineteen fifty-one, a different
kind of dryer reached shoppers:

115
00:06:02,607 --> 00:06:03,677
the bonnet dryer.

116
00:06:04,007 --> 00:06:07,847
A small box blew warm air through
a tube into a bonnet with holes

117
00:06:07,887 --> 00:06:10,717
in it, and the whole thing
could sit on a head at home.

118
00:06:11,172 --> 00:06:15,042
The nineteen fifties were also
the decade of the electric home.

119
00:06:15,532 --> 00:06:18,972
Electric companies sponsored
films that showed families with

120
00:06:19,012 --> 00:06:20,752
new appliances in every room,

121
00:06:20,792 --> 00:06:23,282
and a dryer for hair fit right in.

122
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:31,090
In nineteen fifty-four,

123
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:35,460
the British company GEC changed
the design of the handheld dryer

124
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:38,130
by moving the motor inside the casing.

125
00:06:44,833 --> 00:06:48,153
In the nineteen sixties, better
electric motors and the move

126
00:06:48,193 --> 00:06:50,203
to plastic made dryers lighter,

127
00:06:50,433 --> 00:06:52,243
and that change caught on quickly.

128
00:06:52,744 --> 00:06:55,554
Since the nineteen
twenties, one source notes,

129
00:06:55,924 --> 00:06:58,324
most of the development
has gone into more watts,

130
00:06:58,364 --> 00:07:01,434
and into the materials
and the look of the dryer.

131
00:07:01,976 --> 00:07:03,946
Hood dryers did not disappear.

132
00:07:04,476 --> 00:07:08,266
Salons still use them, now
with a hard plastic dome.

133
00:07:08,536 --> 00:07:12,216
And a handheld dryer in a home
today can use up to two thousand

134
00:07:12,256 --> 00:07:12,786
watts,

135
00:07:13,156 --> 00:07:15,966
twenty times the power of
a nineteen twenties model.

136
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:27,530
A French hairdresser tied
a bonnet to a gas stove.

137
00:07:27,940 --> 00:07:30,310
An American inventor patented a dryer.

138
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:34,300
Around nineteen twenty,
manufacturers shrank the motor until

139
00:07:34,340 --> 00:07:35,550
it fit in a hand.

140
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:39,460
And a machine that once needed a
whole salon now sits in a drawer

141
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:42,690
in the bathroom, and most
of us never think about it.

142
00:07:43,146 --> 00:07:44,336
Ordinary objects.

143
00:07:44,586 --> 00:07:45,956
Extraordinary stories.
